# SHAPE-GATE.allow — this leg's exemptions from the shape gate (bench/tools/shapegate).
# The format, the rules and the shared tooling's entries: bench/SHAPE-GATE.allow.
# Paths are repository-relative.

# NO ENTRIES, AND THE ZERO IS NOT A CLAIM OF VIRTUE. `go run ./bench/tools/shapegate`
# prints no `names` line for bench/tables/rust/src/main.rs, so the exact count
# this ledger would record is 0 and a zero-count entry is not a thing the gate
# can hold (`add` drops a zero, and the ledger check fails an entry whose count
# has dropped). The file is here so the next porter finds the reason rather
# than the absence.
#
# THE REASON, stated because it is a gate blind spot and not a property of this
# leg: the gate's vocabulary matches whole IDENTIFIER TOKENS, and the generated
# Rust API spells the corpus type only inside longer ones —
# `<Name>Row` for the blittable value and `<name>_fixed_load` /
# `<name>_fixed_save` for the entry points. `_` and a following capital are
# word characters, so a token built by suffixing a guarded name is a different
# token and matches nothing. A Rust or Go leg that named a generated symbol
# this way would pass the `names` check even if it were not shape-blind.
#
# THIS LEG IS shape-blind on the merits, which is what the ledger's other
# entries assert of theirs and what a reviewer has to check by reading: the
# runner names the generated type, its plan entry, its read verdict and its two
# generated entry points at ONE `use` and at its storage declarations, and
# nothing else — no field, no pinned value, no wire size. Every size it uses it
# reads from the corpus at run time.
#
# The `consts` check DOES bind this file with no exemption at all, so the
# distinctive wire sizes of these shapes cannot be written down here.
